+.SH BOUNDED INTERFACE
+.EX
+typedef struct JSONText JSONText;
+struct JSONText {
+	ulong n;
+	char *s;
+};
+
+typedef struct JSONLimits JSONLimits;
+struct JSONLimits {
+	ulong maxdepth;
+	ulong maxnodes;
+	ulong maxbytes;
+};
+
+JSON* jsonparsebounded(char *s, ulong n, JSONLimits *limits);
+int   jsonint64(JSON *j, vlong *out);
+char* jsonstring(JSON *j, ulong *n);
+char* jsonkey(JSON *j, JSONEl *e, ulong *n);
+JSON* jsonbynameN(JSON *j, char *s, ulong n);
+long  jsonformat(char *buf, ulong n, JSON *j, int flags);
+int   jsonfmt(Fmt *f, JSON *j, int flags);
+.EE
+.PP
+.I Jsonparsebounded
+parses exactly the supplied readable byte span, which need not have a
+terminating NUL. It rejects malformed UTF-8, malformed JSON number grammar,
+incomplete or unpaired Unicode escapes, duplicate decoded object keys,
+trailing data, and raw NUL bytes. Escaped NUL characters are retained.
+Limits are copied into each parse context; no global policy is changed.
+The root has depth zero. Maximum supported depth is
+.BR JSONMaxDepth " (256)."
+.I Maxnodes
+counts each value and each object key. It must be nonzero.
+.I Maxbytes
+limits cumulative requested allocation bytes, including lexer scratch,
+list cells, text descriptors and terminators, but not allocator overhead.
+This is not a total process-memory, CPU-time or wall-clock limit.
+Input lengths above 0x7FFFFFFF-UTFmax-1 are rejected.
+.PP
+Booleans, null and arrays keep their existing representation.
+Numbers have type
+.B JSONNumberText
+with their exact, allocated number spelling in
+.BR s .
+Strings have type
+.B JSONStringN
+with a pointer
+.B text
+to a counted
+.B JSONText
+in the existing value union.
+The descriptor and its bytes occupy one allocation; its s member points
+immediately after the descriptor. Jsonfree releases that single allocation.
+For a borrowed string view passed only to a formatter, the descriptor and bytes
+may instead be caller-owned; do not pass such a view to jsonfree.
+Objects have type
+.BR JSONObjectN .
+Each element has an extended allocation, JSONElN, containing the unchanged
+JSONEl as its first member and a byte count. The name member still points to
+the decoded key bytes. Jsonkey returns those bytes and their count;
+jsonbynameN looks up a complete counted key. Interior NUL is not a terminator
+for these operations. Existing JSONEl fields and positional initializers
+are unchanged.
+Decoded text is UTF-8 with a convenience terminating NUL, which is not included
+in its count. Interior NUL characters remain part of the counted value.
+.PP
+.I Jsonfree
+releases either representation, including partially built trees on parse error.
+The result owns its text; input storage need not survive the parse call.
+The existing jsonparse entry point continues to return the original types,
+including double-valued JSONNumber. Existing type numbers and structure layouts
+are unchanged. Applications opting into the bounded API must handle its new types.
+.PP
+.I Jsonint64
+converts only JSONNumberText containing a signed 64-bit integer spelling.
+It rejects fractions, exponents and out-of-range values without floating point
+or changing the result on failure. It returns zero on success, -1 on error.
+.I Jsonstring
+returns either string representation with its byte count.
+Jsonstr also accepts JSONStringN, but rejects an interior NUL instead of returning
+a silently truncated C string. Jsonbyname accepts both object representations
+and matches complete keys to its C-string argument.
+.PP
+.I Jsonformat
+formats either representation into the caller's buffer, whose size includes
+the terminating NUL. It returns the byte count excluding that NUL, or -1 if
+the tree, flags or capacity are invalid. Buffer contents on failure must not be
+used; no bytes beyond its capacity are written. Flags are
+.B JSONCompact
+(omit pretty-printing whitespace) and
+.B JSONAscii
+(escape non-ASCII scalars). Counted strings and keys preserve interior NUL.
+Exact numbers are checked and emitted without rounding. The existing %J
+formatter accepts both representations; %#J selects compact output.
+Formatting rejects nesting deeper than JSONMaxDepth. Programmatically built
+trees must have valid, live storage and proper ownership; malformed pointers
+and cyclic lists are not a supported representation.
+.PP
+Parsing/formatting contexts are per call. Independent trees may be processed
+concurrently; callers must synchronize mutation, traversal and freeing of any
+shared tree. Install the global %J formatter before concurrent formatted printing.
+The bounded formatter does not require global formatter registration.
+.PP
+.I Jsonfmt
+uses the same tree printer with a caller-initialized Fmt and the same flags.
+It returns zero on success, -1 on failure, and does not append a terminating NUL.
+The caller owns the formatter buffer, flush policy and output limit, and must
+discard any partially formatted output on failure. This entry point does not
+install or modify the global %J formatter.
